  ok: money always carries a dollar sign, losses included (+$4.20 / -$18.88)
  ok: zero is still money; a missing figure is a dash, never $0.00 -- those mean completely different things on a trading page
  ok: a balance is not signed
  ok: one filled order makes exactly one trade row
  ok: the trade is matched to the LAST signal at or before it, not the first -- pairing it with the earlier look would report a price and a confidence the bot had already moved on from
  ok: profit comes through in dollars, not cents (+$5.26)
  ok: two buys on the SAME market in one close stay two rows with their own prices and their own profit -- merging them is how a scaled-in position gets reported at the wrong price
  ok: a hedged trade shows BOTH legs -- the buy and the hedge
  ok: and the hedge leg is labelled as one
  ok: the two legs add to the real result (-$0.04), not just the winning half -- dropping the hedge leg is how a loss disappears off a dashboard
  ok: every settled leg in the log reaches the page: $-0.04 on the page against $-0.04 in the log
  ok: with a real balance reading the curve is anchored to it
  ok: the real readings appear in the curve untouched
  ok: the EARLIEST point is the first real reading MINUS every profit since -- the curve is walked backwards from a known balance ($294.74)
  ok: and each settled trade puts TWO points on the line, just before and just after, so a loss draws as a cliff rather than a gentle slope between settlements
  ok: the curve is checked against real balance movements
  ok: a $200 deposit the page cannot see is DETECTED (gap $200.00), so the page warns instead of drawing a confident wrong line
  ok: two coins settling on the same second share one close -- hard rule 4, or one bad minute reads as twelve separate disasters
  ok: the page explains the WATCH stage -- the operator asked for 'every single strategy the bot uses ... a buy, a sell, a hedge, every step in the process'
  ok: the page explains the MODEL stage -- the operator asked for 'every single strategy the bot uses ... a buy, a sell, a hedge, every step in the process'
